  they'll call my podcast and they'll say  oh you know i want to retire when i'm 58  and i say great are you ready to finance  35 years of not working because that's  what we're looking at for a lot of  people and so i think the biggest  mistake is underestimating just how long  we'll live  the other problem with the way that we  approach financial planning as women  versus men is that  because women only earn on average 80  percent of what men earn  there are a lot of problems with funding  goals so for example  if you're only earning eighty percent of  what your male compatriot is earning  then you can't save enough for  retirement or as much for retirement and  you will not be in the workforce as long  maybe because you step out to have a  child and be the primary caretaker so i  think that longevity risk risk of just  pay inequality and also that idea of  taking time out of the workforce to  raise a family which can be really  important but it does put you on your  back feet when it comes to your own  financial planning so all these factors  are sort of the big picture items that i  think that many of us would just tend to  overlook as we consider our own  financial futures also too when you look  at your your social security statement  if uh generally speaking women earn less  than men over that long period of time  your social security income is not going  to be the same  as a man's at that same age in life is  that true yeah that's right i mean a  social security system is based on your  work record and if you've taken time out  of the labor force that may mean that  your work record is either not enough to  support yourself or you are ending up  claiming half of your partner's benefits  as your social security benefit which  really it just can be much lower so you  know another mistake that often that  that we'll see with um heterosexual  couples is that the the man says i'm  claiming my social security benefit at  62. i'm just done working the woman may  have been staying home and raising the  family having the primary  responsibilities at home now because her  husband is claiming at age 62 that means  her social security benefit which is  half of his  is  permanently  lower  than  for her lifetime  that's huge let's talk about and there  was a recent fidelity study of women  investors and they they covered the  gamut of age but it showed what it  revealed was that younger women 35 and  under  started investing whether it was  retirement savings uh whether it was  stocks and bonds whatever at generally  an earlier age even as young as 20 as  opposed to their older counterparts 36  and older um  who started later in life does that  surprise you  uh no because it is so much easier to  invest now than for anyone who is over  the age of 35 that there has been a real  democratization of investing and that  investing is as easy as opening up an  app on a phone and plugging in  information so i think that you know the  big advantage you have if you're under  the age of 35 is you've grown up at a  time where there are very low barriers  of entry for the world of investing  whereas you look at a typical say 55  year old woman or man that you go back  in time and you say  did you invest and i think many of us  and i'm in that cohort would be like  invest like i guess maybe through my  retirement account sorta kinda but it  wasn't so easy and i think there has  been a lot more education  and a lot more media attention on how  important it is to start early in your  investing life and you know remember  that gen z and millennials grew up and  came of age where they saw their parents  suffer potentially because they hadn't  saved enough so i think that it's a  really it's a it's a really good trend  that we see  that younger people starting sooner you  know my one caveat is that it's just so  easy to invest that i worry sometimes  that they are kind of going away from  the plain vanilla stuff that will keep  them out of trouble and you know maybe  jumping on the meme stock bandwagon or  buying crypto which is all well and good  but for your long-term investing we  still need you to get as much money as  you can in that roth ira or that roth  401k  right those nfts and all that strange  stuff that's still a little foreign to  those of us  who are in that 35 and over category  indeed  so let's talk about some of the basics  uh  about money makes money um  do you think that people have a good  good idea of concept of compound  interest and how it works well i mean  look the the the long story short about  investing is you've got uh one big goal  is you want to grow your money faster  than the pace of inflation and since  inflation's a big story right now it's  worth underscoring but that's the whole  point of investing and it's interesting  to look at say a few years ago where  people were like not really thinking  about inflation and imagine if you're  not really thinking about inflation the  benefit you have as an investor is that  your money grew by double digits over  the three years coming into 2022 and  that money grew  much faster than the pace of inflation  now that inflation has ticked up to  these 40-year highs you're kind of lucky  that you did that long-term investing  over the last few years because you've  made money in advance of this high  inflationary period so what we know  is that as investors what people need to  understand is that when you put your  money to work what you're trying to do  is look to the future  put your money to work in a basket of  different kinds of assets that could be  stocks that could be bonds that could be  commodities  and that could be real estate investment  trust and you put it all together and  over the long term what you are hoping  to do  is again over the long term at certain  times it's not going to work perfectly  you're trying to beat the rate of  inflation so that your money can grow  when your income can't grow as fast and  when you stop working and it seems like  a younger  the younger generations are a little  more savvy  to what's going on with the economy uh  in the world and inflation and just in  in general economics uh probably more so  maybe  than the 50 and older crowd maybe  and let's face it higher education  uh i was told that  it's on the other side of 50  women in the higher ed uh arena which  does that surprise you well i i think  that what's interesting is that when you  come of age can really determine a lot  of your money habits so first thing that  we know about money and investing and  your habits and where you come to this  place is that a lot of our money  instincts and habits form as early as by  the age seven so we know your family of  origin is going to either screw you up  or help you out a lot in this period  okay so you know if mom was a  spendthrift and mom and dad fought a lot  and there's going to be a lot of  fighting about money it's going to cause  you anxiety and you're gonna bring that  into your adult life i get that okay  that's why the world of psychology  exists okay  but the the interesting thing is that  when you come of age in terms of the era  can also really impact you so i have a  98 year old mother-in-law who was born  in 1923.  her idea and her ideas around investing  were really destroyed because she came  of age amid the great depression and a  world war  and so throughout her adult life you  could have told her over and over again  that she should be a long-term investor  in the stock market and she just  wouldn't be able to do it she was far  too conservative it was too difficult  for her to buy into that and i think  that that's an important thing to  remember and look at me i came of age in  the 80s when the stock market bull  market in stocks began in 1982  i come out of college it's a roaring job  market it's a roaring stock market you  have a lot of belief in this thing  called a retirement plan through work i  wasn't offered a pension it was not  something that was part of what i  thought i was losing i just thought well  i have the ability to invest in this  thing called the stock market which is  doing really well and i have more  confidence in that system fast forward  if you're a millennial and you came of  age in the dot-com boom and bust of the  early 2000s you see your parents suffer  in the housing crisis and the great  recession you live through a pandemic  you may have different thoughts about  risk you may have a different feeling  around what you can absorb and so what's  important to remember is that all these  things shape us it doesn't have to  define us but those feelings shape us  and sometimes you have to struggle  against those feelings and educate  yourself and do try to rely on some part  of this which is the historical context  but also  really being aware that that's my  history that's not the market's history  are you seeing my dog in the background  sorry about that well i'm surprised you  haven't seen mine and and our cat over  here she's had any condo  usually in and out and she usually  sleeps right on that so there you go so  yeah i'm with you there um  i wanted to ask you because you you just  covered can't afford to invest and i i  think that's probably everybody's is  that everybody's individual situation or  or  prospective or can everybody afford to  invest a little bit  look i mean if you're living paycheck to  paycheck you're not investing so let's  be honest there's a good chunk of this  country that is struggling still even if  it even in a hot job market okay  that if you are making uh you know forty  four thousand dollars a year and you're  paying a higher cost of energy a higher  cost of rent a higher cost of food and  you're just worried about making sure  your family is safe and you're paying  your bills chances are you're not  investing right now but i do think that  when you're younger there's this unique  opportunity because you probably can  scour your your monthly income and find  a few bucks to invest you probably can  figure out a way to say oh you know what  think about the pandemic i didn't do a  lot of the normal things i used to do i  was able to look at a extra you know 200  a month or maybe frankly maybe your  student loans have been paused and  they'll be paused again through the  summer maybe that extra couple hundred  bucks every month that you would have  been putting towards your student loans  you may be able to open a roth ira or  participate in your company's retirement  account and get a company match so these  are really good uh gateways to get  yourself in the habit of investing

